00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.320
So the problem statement,
why are we here today?

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.980
We are trying to
talk about how do you

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.170
take your data
warehouse, you know,

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:23.930
on-prem, and move to the
cloud with minimal disruption,

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:25.940
make sure you still
have security,

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.740
and make sure that
all your teams get

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.410
as productive as possible
as quickly as possible?

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.360
So there's certain
elements of this.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.652
And obviously, this is not
going to hold for everybody.

00:01:34.652 --> 00:01:37.580
But there's certain broad
strokes you can look at.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:41.523
So for instance, you have to
look at speed of adoption.

00:01:41.523 --> 00:01:43.190
And one of the key
things is that if you

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.140
have a bunch of
people sitting around,

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.440
and things go
offline, well, it can

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.900
cause significant
disruptions to your business.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.030
So can we get this up quickly?

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.340
Can you get it running quickly?

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.440
And can they be productive
as quickly as possible?

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.000
Then scale, how do
you scale as needed?

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.920
How do you scale elastically?

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.440
So as I mentioned,
Kohl's is my customer.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.780
So in the retail space, you have
a certain seasonality of data.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.370
You have a certain
seasonality of needs of data.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:17.270
And can your data warehouse
help you manage that?

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.677
In other words, can you
scale appropriately?

00:02:19.677 --> 00:02:21.510
And that's really
important because you also

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.330
have to maintain--
not only should you

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.330
be productive with the
data, but you should also

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:28.130
be able to manage your costs.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.960
Insights and
Analysis, how quickly

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.510
can you-- because when
you're moving your data

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.760
warehouse from
on-prem to cloud, will

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.380
your sudden processes
already in place

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.540
to help you be productive
with that data?

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.620
So can you move those workloads
over quickly, efficiently,

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:47.270
and make sure that you can also
empower new kinds of workloads?

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.510
So something more modern comes
along, or something more--

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.050
let's say you are diving
into the AI space.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:59.300
Can your new data warehouse help
you deliver on those workloads?

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.780
And security is paramount.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.210
So you want to make sure that
only people who have access

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.400
to the data should have
access to the data.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.990
You want to make sure that
the needs of your end users

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.040
also are met.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.570
So privacy is maintained.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.625
And these are the
broad strokes when

00:03:16.625 --> 00:03:20.322
it comes to what you need to
consider when moving your data

00:03:20.322 --> 00:03:21.530
warehouse, say, to the cloud.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.330
So most of you are
here in the room

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.790
because you probably
have some, at least,

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.520
familiarity with
how an EDW works.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.020
So this is more of a-- this
is sort of a level set.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:39.517
What is a data warehouse?

00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:41.350
The easiest way to think
of a data warehouse

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.435
is think of it as your
central repository

00:03:43.435 --> 00:03:45.310
where you can just dump
a whole bunch of data

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.590
in so that you can derive
insights out of it.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.420
Now, what it means in
terms of the particular,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.990
will differ from
system to system.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.998
But, in general, that's the
intent of a data warehouse.

00:03:58.998 --> 00:04:00.040
Who is it meant to serve?

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.930
Well, that depends
on your organization.

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:03.805
But in general, you have
a bunch of analysts,

00:04:03.805 --> 00:04:05.020
you have business leaders.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:06.520
You have people
of differing needs

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.970
who need to get insights
out of the data.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.440
Was it it suppose to enable?

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.860
Well, fundamentally, it's
a driver of business.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.670
That's the key requirement
of collecting the data

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.500
and analyzing the data
in the first place.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:21.408
So you cannot keep--

00:04:21.408 --> 00:04:23.200
that is one of the
first things that you've

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.840
got to keep in mind is
that how do you enable

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:29.690
business decisions to be made?

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.183
And what it can
look like, so I've

00:04:34.183 --> 00:04:36.100
got in a bunch of arguments
with a whole bunch

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.990
of people about what does a--

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:40.250
what is a representative
slide for this?

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:44.390
So none of us could decide
as to a common theme,

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:49.150
so we decided, you know what,
let's go the magic of Wikipedia

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.860
where are a whole bunch of
other people can argue about it,

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.360
and come up with something
that is rather simple.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.110
As you can see, you've
got a bunch of data

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.000
on the left-hand side.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.290
It gets in the staging kind
of area, and gets ingested.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:03.830
And you have
different data mods,

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.000
which are aligned to
specific business needs.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.270
And you have different purposes
that those data mods in turn

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:12.400
could serve.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.910
So it's, again,
large volumes of data

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:18.990
that you wrangle, get
it into a central area,

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.030
and now that can
be piped through--

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:23.890
to serve different
business needs,

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:27.547
and different people can gain
different insights out of it,

00:05:27.547 --> 00:05:29.380
make different business
decisions out of it.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.010
So now, we talked about
just what an EDW is.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.530
And one of the big
reasons that we

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:45.263
are talking about why do you
want to move to the cloud,

00:05:45.263 --> 00:05:46.680
well, there's some
reasons for it.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.030
Because if there weren't
any issues or challenges,

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.200
you wouldn't need to do the
migration in the first place.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.970
So, one of the
first things that we

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.160
have to deal with
in today's world

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.290
is that the amount of
data and the kinds of data

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.390
you're dealing with
is rapidly changing.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:06.480
So not only do you
have to accommodate

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.870
for the amount of
data, but the kinds

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.660
of data, and the speed
at which it's ingested.

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.430
Now, one way of handling
this is sort of provision

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.082
on your data warehouse.

00:06:19.082 --> 00:06:20.790
This is where the
seasonality of the data

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.110
actually has a
significant impact.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.712
Because if you over-scale
to handle the spikes,

00:06:26.712 --> 00:06:28.920
well, now you're stuck with
a bunch of infrastructure

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.460
sitting around that
is underutilized

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.350
most of the time,
which is not efficient.

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.960
The other way is
you under-provision,

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.720
and say, let's just
ignore the spikes.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.780
And that's not going
to work, either.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:44.730
Is it responsive to the needs?

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:45.690
How does it adapt?

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.910
As you have more and more
people coming in, say,

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:53.700
with different ideas, and
they want to try out stuff,

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:56.160
can your data warehouse
accommodate their needs

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.410
as well?

00:06:58.410 --> 00:06:59.310
Can it be faster?

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.710
Can you produce your reports
faster so that people

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:04.560
can make decisions faster?

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.653
Different kinds of workloads,
so in some cases, your--

00:07:08.653 --> 00:07:11.070
let's say that you're doing a
bunch of aggregate analysis,

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:11.970
great.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:15.860
But in some cases, say you want
to create a new data set out

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:19.590
of it that is more suitable
towards, say, machine learning.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.218
So, can it actually
accommodate that?

00:07:24.218 --> 00:07:26.010
The other thing that
I've seen in the field

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:29.595
is just that it's a
victim of its own success.

00:07:29.595 --> 00:07:32.160
When you bring all the
data in, you go in,

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.140
and you make this
significant investment,

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:38.580
and now you have this central
repository that is enabling

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.680
all these business decisions.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.640
Well, the expectation is that it
continues to do that over time.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.220
And now suddenly, two years
down the road, you say,

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:50.960
we need more
capacity, or we need--

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.500
it's no longer providing
the kind of results

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.150
that we are used to.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.950
So it's almost as if it
was transformational.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.950
But because now the
expectation's so high,

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:07.290
it's hard to keep up, especially
in a staticky kind of setup

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:10.530
that a traditional
on-prem is going to be.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.390
And then-- which goes
to can it meet our needs

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:15.237
two, three five years from now?

00:08:15.237 --> 00:08:17.070
Are you going to have
the same conversations

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:19.570
over and over again with
your business leaders saying,

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.500
oh, we need more capacity?

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:25.180
Or we can't run these
workloads, we can't--

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.977
can it still deliver over time?

00:08:28.977 --> 00:08:30.810
And so these are some
of the common refrains

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.150
that we see in the field
all the time, which

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:38.520
is why I would like to now
bring in Jas from Kohl's, who

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.220
went through this in real life.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.530
JASVINDER SINGH:
So, thank you Sam.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.200
First of all, my introduction.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.210
I am Jasvinder Singh.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.960
I'm a senior manager of big
data analytics at Kohl's.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.730
And thank you,
Sam, for reminding

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.850
that we have to go back
to the 40-degree weather

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.430
after enjoying the
70-degree weather over here.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:32.700
So in most of the enterprises,
the analytical ecosystem

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:36.360
has been built over
past several years.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.910
And over the years, we have
collected different systems.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.760
And more recently in
the last few years,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.930
most of the companies have
also added big data and Hadoop

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:50.740
to their ecosystem.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.720
So let's talk about why did
we even need so many systems

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.050
to start with?

00:09:56.050 --> 00:09:59.500
So the main factor was cost.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:05.220
So as we brought in
these technical stacks,

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:08.420
and they had kind
of fixed capacity.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:12.180
So based on the cost and
based on our use cases,

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:15.070
we bought some fixed capacity.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:18.120
And as we started working
through the years,

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.910
the capacity was not
enough because we

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:24.160
needed to solve more
and more use cases.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.220
and so we ended up bringing more
of these monolithic solutions.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:33.385
And some of those solutions
that were brought in

00:10:33.385 --> 00:10:36.870
were for our technical
capabilities,

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.180
some of the use cases
needed a different system

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.660
to be brought in.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.840
So at the end of
the day, we had data

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.750
coming in from
our source systems

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:49.990
and landing into different
warehousing systems.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:55.380
And since we could not bring
data into a single system,

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:57.570
we basically had the
need of exchanging data

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.300
between the systems,
so a lot of data

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.490
was being copied
across these systems.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:05.640
And at the end of the
day, we had basically

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:10.160
arrows going in every
direction that you can imagine.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.780
So just to highlight what
are the issues that we deal

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:16.450
with with an
architecture like this,

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:20.470
we had disparate data
sources for enterprise use.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.080
We had inconsistent analysis.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.850
And decisions were inconsistent
because the data was

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:31.660
in different systems, and as
it was copied from one system

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.290
to another system, some
of the data quality things

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:40.130
were also having issues with.

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.590
It was difficult
to access the data

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:45.010
across different platforms.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.330
So let's say you needed data
from two of the systems,

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:48.860
and I have to build a report.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:51.290
The only way I could
do that is basically

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:55.280
copy the data from one
system to other system.

00:11:55.280 --> 00:12:00.680
And also it was very difficult
to understand the other level

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.720
data within these systems.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.250
And if you're
looking for data, it

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:06.930
was kind of a scavenger hunt.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:11.090
And there is very few people
in the enterprise who know

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:13.310
where to get the data from.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:14.810
And the other
problem that we were

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:18.440
having was that there was
no common understanding

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:23.060
of the definitions and the
measurements of the data

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.970
within the warehousing systems.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:30.260
So overall, we had
a big opportunity

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:34.190
that we could basically
go for and solve it

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:37.010
in our future use cases.

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:40.340
So, in our traditional
warehousing system,

00:12:40.340 --> 00:12:46.280
we have these characteristics
that are limitations.

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:50.540
So you can see on
the left-hand side,

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.810
we had to-- as we brought
in data into our ecosystem,

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:55.880
we had to structure it first.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.610
And the reason we had
to structure it first is

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:03.080
because we cannot just bring
in anything and everything,

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:06.600
and the reason being it is
basically fixed capacity.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.580
So there was a lot of time
spent upfront to structure

00:13:10.580 --> 00:13:13.820
the data before we
ingested it and brought it

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.640
for the analytical purposes.

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:19.430
And over the years,
as I said, we

00:13:19.430 --> 00:13:21.800
brought these monolithic stacks.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:26.660
And as we are working
through different use cases,

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:29.040
one stack would not
solve the problem.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.850
So we had to bring
in some other stack

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:36.240
to solve the newer use cases.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.370
So at the end of
the day, we had--

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.250
some of our challenges
on-prem were

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:45.780
we had a very complex
operations in our data centers.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:50.100
We had these multiple stacks
which were just talking

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.710
to each other, exchanging data.

00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:56.790
And it was becoming
more complex for us

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:00.510
to support in our data center.

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:03.750
And even with all of
these different stacks,

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:09.080
it was very difficult to
keep up with the data growth.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.380
So our new approach,
or the new way

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:16.360
of delivering the
warehouse, is that we

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:20.800
are going with a
flexible structure

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.820
where we can get in
any kind of data.

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:26.010
So we can basically
get in structured data,

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.720
semi-structured, or
unstructured data,

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:33.610
and we can ingest it, and then
analyze it, and then structure

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.950
it based on our needs.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:40.460
And the ecosystem will
be of dynamic capacities,

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:42.730
or we can add
capacity as needed.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:46.630
And it is an ecosystem
which is enabled

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:50.800
by the bunch of services which
can basically solve all our use

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:53.920
cases rather than just
moving the data from one

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:55.000
system to other system.

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:02.850
So in terms of
advantages, we basically

00:15:02.850 --> 00:15:07.090
had a faster speed to market.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:09.130
We basically enabled
a bunch of services,

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:11.440
and we were able
to quickly stand

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:14.210
up and deliver some value.

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:18.310
Our new approach is
elastic and also managed

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:20.590
so that we don't
have to deal with

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:23.680
all these complex
infrastructure operations.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:31.230
So here is how we
are planning to build

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:38.460
the single source of truth
in the Google Cloud BigQuery.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:43.640
So this is basically Kohl's
data lake in BigQuery.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.200
So as you can see, we
have different systems

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:49.420
on the left-hand side,
similar to what we had before.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:51.070
And we have data coming in.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.220
It can be any kind
of data, whether it's

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:55.130
a batch or real-time.

00:15:55.130 --> 00:15:59.370
And we are assembling all of
the data in Google BigQuery,

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:02.790
and then making the
data available for all

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.170
of our use cases here.

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:09.810
So some of the benefits of using
an architecture like this is we

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:13.020
have basically a
single source of truth.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:17.370
And all the analytical use
cases are driving the value

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:23.940
from the single
warehouse in BigQuery.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.810
So also using this architecture,
we have enabled self-service.

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.900
So we have provided
the decision makers

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:34.800
with easy access and
readily-available data

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:38.530
which is secure and clean.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:43.150
Along with clean data, we have
provided our business users

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.780
with some data discovery
and visualization

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:50.130
tools, which will help them
with speeding up the decision

00:16:50.130 --> 00:16:51.790
process.

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:55.500
And since the team is not busy
with just copying the data

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:58.020
from one system to
other system, we

00:16:58.020 --> 00:17:03.040
have started focusing on some
of the advanced analytics.

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:08.130
We have started-- shifted
from structured reporting

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:12.060
to kind of more
actionable insights

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:14.839
and predictive analytics.

00:17:14.839 --> 00:17:18.270
And since, as you can see, the
data is in the single system,

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.540
we are now standardizing
the business metadata,

00:17:21.540 --> 00:17:24.240
and the attributes,
and the metrics

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.420
across the organization.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.930
So our approach for building
this has been very simple.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:34.250
So we have been building a
parallel environment in Google

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:38.480
by channeling all of
our existing sources

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:42.110
that are going into the
traditional warehouse today

00:17:42.110 --> 00:17:45.590
and channeling them
to go into BigQuery.

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:49.190
So as we bring and land all
of the data into BigQuery,

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:52.580
we are working with
our business partners

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:57.650
and prioritizing how they
would like to cleanse and start

00:17:57.650 --> 00:17:59.990
using the data within BigQuery.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:08.860
So I think-- so as
we bring the data,

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:11.620
and we cleanse it,
make it available,

00:18:11.620 --> 00:18:13.450
we have started to
solve certain use

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:19.510
cases that were being solved
with our previous solution

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:20.570
as well.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.920
But now we are able to
solve some other use

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:27.430
cases which totally
needed a different stack.

00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:30.520
Marketing operations is
also leveraging this stack,

00:18:30.520 --> 00:18:34.870
and also recommendations can
also leverage this stack.

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:36.760
And as we are
delivering the data,

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.470
we are also doing
some comparison

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:40.570
between on-prem and cloud.

00:18:40.570 --> 00:18:45.160
And we have tools that we have
built to showcase that the data

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:46.830
which we are--

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:51.100
which we are assembling in
BigQuery is higher quality,

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:55.610
and also it is delivered
with lesser amount of time.

00:18:55.610 --> 00:18:59.250
And it can be leveraged for
analytics plus other use cases

00:18:59.250 --> 00:18:59.750
as well.

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:09.400
So here is the logical
architecture slide

00:19:09.400 --> 00:19:12.220
that we have been using.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:14.920
So going from bottom
to the top, we

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:18.830
have different sources
of data coming in.

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:21.850
We have external
data sources, and we

00:19:21.850 --> 00:19:23.900
have internal data sources.

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:27.400
So we are landing the data
using either file transports,

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:32.380
or reading real time but Plume,
or getting it into Kafka.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:34.000
And we are landing
all of the data

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:37.600
into a BigQuery
staging area where

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:40.960
we are applying some
transformation logic, some

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:42.940
of the business
logic, and making

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:46.150
the data available in
the certified data sets.

00:19:46.150 --> 00:19:50.290
And in some cases, we might
be creating some enterprise

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:52.300
reporting structures
which are being

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:55.850
leveraged by the
different reporting tools.

00:19:55.850 --> 00:19:59.590
So I think the key thing
here is that all of this

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:01.700
is being done in BigQuery.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:05.260
So we are not really
leveraging a lot of other tools

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:06.790
to deliver the data.

00:20:06.790 --> 00:20:10.540
Although you can see that we do
have a little bit of Dataproc.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:14.590
And that is being leveraged for
some advanced transformation

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.440
use cases or certain
use cases where

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:19.510
we have to protect
the data differently.

00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:22.450
So we do have some
Dataproc and Spark

00:20:22.450 --> 00:20:24.290
that we are leveraging for that.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:30.450
But generally speaking, those
use cases are less than 5%.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.950
So using this architecture,
we are basically

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:37.250
enabling faster access
to the data for Kohl's

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:39.530
analytical communities.

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:41.720
We have increased the
speed and efficiency

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:43.430
of the data delivery.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:48.470
And also since it is a
single kind of a stack,

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:51.620
it is very easy to
facilitate the governance

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:54.530
and ensure the data quality.

00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:59.000
And we are using data security
using some of the encryption

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:03.650
that BigQuery provides,
and also managing access

00:21:03.650 --> 00:21:08.060
using the fine-grained access
control that are in BigQuery.

00:21:08.060 --> 00:21:11.540
So overall, this is
standardizing the data

00:21:11.540 --> 00:21:15.825
architecture across Kohl's
analytical communities.

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:25.540
So at a high level,
the data that we

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:30.550
are bringing into our warehouse
is primarily real-time sources,

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:35.730
or it is the bad sources
that are coming in.

00:21:35.730 --> 00:21:40.810
So in case of the bad
sources, we basically

00:21:40.810 --> 00:21:45.190
land the data in Google
Storage in the raw state.

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:48.820
And as I discussed
in my previous slide,

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:52.570
most of the data can
directly go into BigQuery.

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:57.540
And in some cases, we
are using some Spark,

00:21:57.540 --> 00:21:59.540
and it's a lightweight
application,

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:04.660
which can help it with certain
PII elements protection,

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:08.380
converting the formats
if the formats are not

00:22:08.380 --> 00:22:11.410
ready for BigQuery,
or converting it

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.460
to a different file format.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:16.150
So once the data
is loaded into raw,

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:18.820
and you can see a similar
pipeline on the top

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:22.310
where we have streaming data
coming in from the sources,

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:24.280
whether they are
read through APIs,

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:29.090
or these are pushed into our
Kafka kind of infrastructure,

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:31.690
and we have a Spark
streaming application which

00:22:31.690 --> 00:22:34.690
is using the BigQuery
streaming API

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:40.300
and landing the data in raw in
a streaming fashion in BigQuery.

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:42.650
So once the data
is all in the raw,

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:48.860
we are combining the data set,
creating the gold structures

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:50.720
or the certified data sets.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:54.410
And once the data is available
in the gold data sets,

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:57.690
it is available for
different use cases,

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:01.520
whether it is the data
scientists who are basically

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:04.520
pulling the data using
some BiSpark jobs,

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:07.340
using BQ's storage APIs.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:15.520
Or we have extracts that are
being run through a Spark job,

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:17.800
or there are some
reporting use cases which

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:21.700
we are enabling on Tableau,
and all the Ad-Hoc analytics

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.070
is basically
happening on BigQuery.

00:23:27.070 --> 00:23:33.410
So to summarize here,
so this talks about some

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:35.120
of the challenges
that we were having

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:37.560
with our on-prem solution.

00:23:37.560 --> 00:23:40.910
So we have-- like with our
current on-prem solution, where

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:46.010
we Hadoop and we had
[INAUDIBLE] systems,

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:49.580
we had very complex operations.

00:23:49.580 --> 00:23:53.300
And then that-- and
also with that kind

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:56.480
of complex operations,
we were unable to keep up

00:23:56.480 --> 00:23:58.070
with the data growth.

00:23:58.070 --> 00:24:00.680
And we were having
issues with data

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:06.710
latency due to the data
moving across various systems.

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:10.700
We had a very complex
implementation.

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:13.070
Since we had kind of
a zoo of technologies

00:24:13.070 --> 00:24:18.820
and no common approaches, it was
very difficult to just manage

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:20.540
the ecosystem.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.820
Data was hard to understand.

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:24.650
And the data was--

00:24:24.650 --> 00:24:26.660
the data quality was
suffering because it was

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:28.460
going through multiple hops.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:33.530
And the data existed
in multiple systems.

00:24:33.530 --> 00:24:35.880
And our infrastructure
cost was really high

00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:38.940
because there was lot of
duplication that was happening.

00:24:42.270 --> 00:24:47.460
So the advantages of doing this
in the cloud BigQuery ecosystem

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:49.980
is that we don't have
any upfront cost.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.110
And as we need--

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.390
as we are adding more
and more to BigQuery,

00:24:54.390 --> 00:24:59.160
we are adding more capacity to
our BigQuery infrastructure.

00:24:59.160 --> 00:25:07.798
And it is-- so we leverage
the fixed slots within Kohl's.

00:25:07.798 --> 00:25:09.840
So we started off with a
certain number of slots.

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:13.570
And as we are growing,
storage is not a problem.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:15.750
But as we need more
and more compute,

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:19.680
we are working with
our Google, and we

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:22.135
are able to bring in
more slots as needed.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:30.410
So it's a faster speed
to market because

00:25:30.410 --> 00:25:33.590
of usage of the cloud services.

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.710
And also as we are
bringing in data,

00:25:36.710 --> 00:25:41.390
we have created some reusable
components and functions

00:25:41.390 --> 00:25:46.250
that can help with file
management or auditing.

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:48.230
So those are kind
of-- just everything

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:49.380
is a template for us.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:51.830
So as we bring in
more and more data,

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:55.910
we just use a standard template,
and we bring in all the data.

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:59.930
And some of those standard
functions are being used.

00:25:59.930 --> 00:26:03.500
The solution is elastic from
both compute and storage

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:04.730
standpoint.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:08.460
And it is hosted by Google.

00:26:08.460 --> 00:26:14.030
So with that, I bring Sam
back to the podium again.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:15.022
Thank you, Sam.

00:26:20.478 --> 00:26:21.980
SAMRAT SAHA: Hello?

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:22.960
All right.

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:25.000
So thanks, Jas.

00:26:25.000 --> 00:26:28.690
As you can see, he has
the breadth and depth

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:31.720
of expertise in
managing data at scale

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:33.700
for a really large retailer.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:36.190
And I've been fortunate to
be part of that journey.

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:38.710
And I've seen how
complex it can be.

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:40.600
Sometimes it can
be overwhelming.

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:43.010
You have tons of
different moving parts,

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:44.860
and you have something
already in place.

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:48.118
So the ante's
really high, right,

00:26:48.118 --> 00:26:50.410
is that you're going from
something that's functional--

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:53.938
yes, you may not be
extremely satisfied with it--

00:26:53.938 --> 00:26:55.480
and you're trying
to move all of that

00:26:55.480 --> 00:26:58.180
to a completely different
system altogether

00:26:58.180 --> 00:27:00.720
without disrupting business.

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:07.920
So this is why I say,
what just happened,

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.680
because there was a lot
of moving parts there.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:16.440
And in my experience with
working with Jas and his team,

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:18.300
how do you take a
monumental task like this?

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:21.663
And in our discussions, he
had a very insightful way

00:27:21.663 --> 00:27:23.080
of discussing it
or describing it.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:24.700
This is a generational event.

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:26.550
It doesn't happen all the time.

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:28.850
So when you are
there, this is not--

00:27:28.850 --> 00:27:31.020
this is going to be a
"once in a generation"

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.660
event that you're going to take
something, your existing data

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:35.700
warehouse, and move
it to the cloud.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:39.210
So it is complex, and you have
a lot of different people.

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:42.060
And you have to get buy-in
from a whole bunch of people.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:43.500
How do you manage that?

00:27:43.500 --> 00:27:45.820
Obviously, leverage
accounting, this

00:27:45.820 --> 00:27:47.670
is where people like me come in.

00:27:47.670 --> 00:27:49.410
We can help out.

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:52.230
If nothing else, when
you get frustrated,

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:53.260
you can just yell at me.

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:54.390
That's cool.

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:57.900
But it comes down to this.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:28:01.110
The first thing is you
have to decide to move.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:06.030
It is a decision that has to--
you have to be firm about it.

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:07.580
It's not a wishy-washy thing.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:10.060
Let's go for it.

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:11.760
You have to get
stakeholder buy-in.

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:12.775
That's one of the--

00:28:12.775 --> 00:28:14.560
in a complex
transformation like this,

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:17.890
you need to get buy-in
across the board.

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:21.070
So that is key.

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:23.590
Then one of the things
that I saw Jas' team do

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.860
is you go through diligently and
identify all the data sources,

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:29.380
auditing it, making sure
that they all make sense,

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:33.540
figuring out what is it going
to look like in, say, BigQuery

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:36.730
because they use BigQuery as
the central pillar of their data

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:39.290
strategy going forward.

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:43.410
Then the simplest thing
is identify a chunk.

00:28:43.410 --> 00:28:46.470
Go the POC route so that
you can get-- first of all,

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:48.980
it allows your team
to get comfortable

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:52.520
with the new semantics, the new
technology, the new paradigms

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:56.210
that are involved in
BigQuery, and build upon it.

00:28:56.210 --> 00:29:00.950
It allows you to re-imagine
what your data warehouse is

00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:04.800
going to be in Google Cloud.

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:06.980
And obviously, execute.

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:10.190
And I've seen them execute
on this, and flawlessly.

00:29:10.190 --> 00:29:12.590
It has been complex.

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:16.980
But his team has done
an awesome job on this.

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:19.050
So in one of the
previous slides,

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:22.040
I said, what are the
broad strokes that you

00:29:22.040 --> 00:29:25.490
need to consider when
you are moving your data

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:27.240
warehouse to the cloud?

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:28.620
That was speed of adoption.

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:29.540
So this is BigQuery.

00:29:29.540 --> 00:29:34.490
This is our solution to help
you be data informed at scale.

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:38.690
So speed of adoption,
BigQuery, you

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:40.700
can get up and
running very quickly.

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:43.440
If your analysts are very
comfortable using SQL,

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:43.940
go for it.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.030
It's available for you.

00:29:46.030 --> 00:29:48.560
As Jas just mentioned,
scaling as needed.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:50.840
So they started off with
a bunch of fixed slots,

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.218
and as their capacity
needs started increasing,

00:29:53.218 --> 00:29:55.010
they started increasing
the number of slots

00:29:55.010 --> 00:29:56.270
available to them.

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:58.480
So they could scale as needed.

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:00.620
Insights analysis,
again, they moved over

00:30:00.620 --> 00:30:03.590
some of the existing scripts,
some of the existing workloads,

00:30:03.590 --> 00:30:05.420
and were productive as
quickly as possible.

00:30:05.420 --> 00:30:08.300
And they also unlocked
more workloads.

00:30:08.300 --> 00:30:10.940
And they were able to
deliver on this faster

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:13.400
over much more amounts of data.

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:17.360
And it's also opening
up-- and there's

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:19.450
an article on
BigQuery, is that ML is

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:20.700
going more and more important.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:22.790
So BQML, if you're
not familiar with it,

00:30:22.790 --> 00:30:24.770
I would highly recommend
looking into it.

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.380
It allows you to execute machine
learning models on BigQuery

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:29.630
itself, which is pretty cool.

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:32.750
And security, encryption
at rest, in flight,

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:36.630
and you've got very fine-grained
access controls using IAM.

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:39.440
And with this-- some
customers go ahead

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:40.530
and do it on their own.

00:30:40.530 --> 00:30:42.680
They have their deep expertise.

00:30:42.680 --> 00:30:43.790
Their teams are built out.

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:45.498
They can actually pull
this on their own.

00:30:45.498 --> 00:30:48.050
And sometimes, you
need additional help.

00:30:48.050 --> 00:30:50.710
And this is why at
GCP we allow you--

00:30:50.710 --> 00:30:53.388
we try to help you
figure out what is

00:30:53.388 --> 00:30:54.680
the best approach you can take.

00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:56.240
And sometimes, you
need a partner.

00:30:56.240 --> 00:31:01.970
And this is where I will
look introduce Dr. Phil, who

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:03.353
is the president of Datametica.

00:31:06.071 --> 00:31:09.180
PHIL SHELLEY: Thanks Sam.

00:31:09.180 --> 00:31:10.280
Thanks Jas, too.

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:13.590
So let's get into
migrating data warehouses.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:14.618
So why move to GCP?

00:31:14.618 --> 00:31:16.160
You've already
covered a lot of that.

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:19.070
There's some very good reasons
to move analytics workload

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:19.770
to GCP.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:21.930
It's the best in
class in our view.

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:23.500
BQ is a killer app.

00:31:23.500 --> 00:31:26.070
And I think many of you
know about it already.

00:31:26.070 --> 00:31:27.480
But moving a large
data warehouse

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:29.930
has some risks and
challenges to the business.

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:32.970
The business can be
clearly severely damaged.

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:35.550
If you scrub the migration
of a data warehouse,

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:37.810
you're going to know
about it pretty quickly.

00:31:37.810 --> 00:31:40.620
So we started years ago
moving these things manually.

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:42.900
But you take a large,
complex data warehouse

00:31:42.900 --> 00:31:44.850
or an infrastructure
like Jas presented,

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:46.650
it's extremely
complex to understand

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:50.280
the data flows, contingencies,
their interdependencies.

00:31:50.280 --> 00:31:52.782
And to move that
manually is risky

00:31:52.782 --> 00:31:54.240
because you can
move a little piece

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:56.320
and then break the rest
of it in the process.

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:59.430
So we started to figure
out how do we move this

00:31:59.430 --> 00:32:03.210
in a more automated fashion
quite a few years ago, and then

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:04.860
how do we actually manage--

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:05.967
this is a wordy slide.

00:32:05.967 --> 00:32:07.050
Sorry about it's so wordy.

00:32:07.050 --> 00:32:09.690
But you can take
your time to read it.

00:32:09.690 --> 00:32:11.580
But really the reason
to go to slide--

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:14.490
to cloud is the
unlimited scalability,

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:17.100
the flexibility of
performance you get,

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:20.430
the ability to bring in all
the different data sets--

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:23.970
streaming, real-time,
IoT, batch,

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:26.700
and structured, unstructured
in any sequence,

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:31.160
any format you want, and the
speed of latency that you need.

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:33.570
And putting all your data
in a single point of truth,

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:37.380
there's no question, we all know
that's logical, sensible, very

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:41.880
helpful, and is definitely the
direction that we're all going.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:43.560
The fast pace of
features that are

00:32:43.560 --> 00:32:46.770
being added you see constantly,
or the announcements at NEXT,

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:49.200
here, new features coming
all the time, there's

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:51.750
no way you can--
anyone else can compete

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:54.940
with the speed of cloud vendors'
innovation at the moment.

00:32:54.940 --> 00:32:58.030
And the new capabilities
are coming every few weeks.

00:32:58.030 --> 00:33:00.330
And because you're
on one platform

00:33:00.330 --> 00:33:03.420
now with the data in a
single point of truth place,

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:06.610
you can start to adopt those
tools as they come along.

00:33:06.610 --> 00:33:08.420
And then you can
focus, as Jas said,

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:12.090
you can focus more on the
actual workload innovation.

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:13.710
How do you drive
value from the data,

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:15.570
rather than worrying
about running

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:18.900
servers, headaches of
data centers blowing up

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:21.270
in the middle of the night,
which Keith and I had

00:33:21.270 --> 00:33:23.190
a few years ago.

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:25.350
It's just getting you
out of that headache

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:28.740
and focusing on the
value from your data

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:31.340
and adopt these new
tools as they come.

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:33.110
So cost is a big play.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:36.210
So you're starting to talk
about large tower data

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:41.440
appliances of $20 million
plus each, in some cases

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:44.790
five to six years life, 20%
annual maintenance on top

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:49.410
of that, massive costs
of buying and acquiring,

00:33:49.410 --> 00:33:51.490
and then all the
people to run that.

00:33:51.490 --> 00:33:53.490
So the cost advantages
of moving to cloud

00:33:53.490 --> 00:33:54.480
are pretty significant.

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:57.810
We often do cost
calculations for companies

00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:01.710
early on to show
the cost benefit.

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:06.060
And in the larger data
warehouses, we see up to 60%--

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:11.219
almost 70% in some cases
five-year cost savings.

00:34:11.219 --> 00:34:14.830
Even in the small ones, we
see 20% to 30% cost savings.

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:17.790
So going to cloud
is very powerful.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:20.770
And I'm going to come back
on that in a little while.

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:23.070
And then you pay for
what you use, of course.

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:25.739
Also we want to eliminate
a lot of this legacy ETL.

00:34:25.739 --> 00:34:29.750
We've had ETL around for 30
years or more in some cases.

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:31.409
It's very batch-wise thinking.

00:34:31.409 --> 00:34:33.600
It's not the modern way
of dealing with data.

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:36.030
IoT, streaming,
Jas mentioned that.

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:37.980
The way we're going is
real-time enterprise.

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:39.690
Streaming is happening.

00:34:39.690 --> 00:34:42.600
It's something we have to
embrace because the old ETL

00:34:42.600 --> 00:34:45.159
mentality is really
holding us back.

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:48.659
So we want to eliminate a lot
of legacy ETL at the same time,

00:34:48.659 --> 00:34:51.090
and we want to be
more labor efficient.

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:53.820
So common challenges
when you want

00:34:53.820 --> 00:34:56.699
to move a data warehouse so that
you have to address internally

00:34:56.699 --> 00:34:59.160
is budget and cost, of course.

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:01.110
It's going to cost
something to move.

00:35:01.110 --> 00:35:05.160
You will be paid back within
a year, or two, or three.

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:07.500
At most typically you
will see a payback.

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:10.170
Sometimes even in a single
year, you'll see payback.

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.570
Especially if your data
where house is end of life.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:16.080
If it's about to be
end-of-life, the ROI on moving

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:20.455
is very powerful, usually
less than a year of payback.

00:35:20.455 --> 00:35:21.580
So how did you get started?

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:22.740
What are the steps?

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:24.173
How did you get your data there?

00:35:24.173 --> 00:35:25.840
Not only how did you
get the data there,

00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:27.340
but how do you keep
it synchronized?

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:29.670
Because this is a moving train.

00:35:29.670 --> 00:35:31.283
Your existing system is live.

00:35:31.283 --> 00:35:32.700
There's data flowing
all the time.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:33.780
It's changing.

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:35.340
So you can't just
take a snapshot

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:36.960
and move it and
expect it to work.

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:38.540
It doesn't work like that.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:40.290
So how did you move
it all, and how do you

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:41.850
keep it all synchronized?

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:45.480
And it can take years to do this
because nobody has the patients

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:48.000
to do a big multi-year
project anymore.

00:35:48.000 --> 00:35:49.675
It has to take weeks and months.

00:35:49.675 --> 00:35:51.300
So those are the
challenges you'll see.

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:55.300
So we started to develop years
ago some tools around that.

00:35:55.300 --> 00:35:57.390
First of all, how
do you assess--

00:35:57.390 --> 00:35:59.880
how do you plan and assess this?

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:03.610
I can-- if you all here
have a data warehouse,

00:36:03.610 --> 00:36:06.270
I can pretty much guarantee you
don't know everything that's

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:07.930
running on your data warehouse.

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:09.250
There are jobs--

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:12.730
ETL jobs, transformation
jobs, user jobs.

00:36:12.730 --> 00:36:13.950
There's data being adjusted.

00:36:13.950 --> 00:36:16.620
I can promise you you
don't know and you have not

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:19.517
documented everything that's
running on your system.

00:36:19.517 --> 00:36:21.850
So how can you move it if you
don't know what's running?

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:23.480
So we developed a tool.

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:27.330
It's a machine learning-based
tool that discovers everything,

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:29.220
I mean, literally
everything that's

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:32.942
running on an environment, so
in a very fine-grained detail.

00:36:32.942 --> 00:36:34.650
And then it uses the
clustering algorithm

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:37.170
to cluster pieces
of workload together

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:41.150
so you can move a cluster
at a time in a sprint model.

00:36:41.150 --> 00:36:43.820
The next piece is how do
you convert all the code?

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:48.590
You can't just typically
lift and shift a code base

00:36:48.590 --> 00:36:49.990
from a data warehouse.

00:36:49.990 --> 00:36:53.960
So if you took Teradata, it may
have M loads, it may have SQL.

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:56.330
For users it might
have BTEQ scripts,

00:36:56.330 --> 00:37:00.090
it might have ETL scripts
that are non-standard SQL.

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:02.900
And then you're going to
put them onto BQ to run,

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:05.360
or on Spark, or
whatever on cloud.

00:37:05.360 --> 00:37:08.330
They have to be
converted in some cases.

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:11.150
So rarely can you--
almost never can

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:13.398
you lift and shift
a data warehouse

00:37:13.398 --> 00:37:15.440
with all of the workload
without some conversion.

00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:17.450
So how do you automate
that conversion?

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:19.850
How would you convert
different dialects of SQL

00:37:19.850 --> 00:37:22.820
to a standardized
BQ SQL or Spark SQL?

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:25.310
So again, we have
to automate that.

00:37:25.310 --> 00:37:26.660
Then how do you move the data?

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:27.870
So how did you move--

00:37:27.870 --> 00:37:29.250
how did you replace that ETL?

00:37:29.250 --> 00:37:31.770
How do you replace
the data pipelines?

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:35.570
How do we make that a more
seamless parametrically-driven

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:36.170
exercise?

00:37:36.170 --> 00:37:38.420
And finally, how do
you validate the data?

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:40.070
As you in transition,
you've got data

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:43.340
in one data warehouse
that's a live system,

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:46.010
you've now got data
in cloud, and you've

00:37:46.010 --> 00:37:48.090
got to run in parallel
for a period of time

00:37:48.090 --> 00:37:50.490
because you need to know
those two systems match.

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:52.615
You need to know that your
new system is giving you

00:37:52.615 --> 00:37:54.890
exactly the same results
as the old system.

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:56.630
With the same data
inputs, you're

00:37:56.630 --> 00:37:59.090
getting the same data
outputs because your business

00:37:59.090 --> 00:38:03.150
users won't let you switch over
until each phase is validated.

00:38:03.150 --> 00:38:06.060
So validation is
a huge challenge.

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:07.550
So we have automated
that as well.

00:38:07.550 --> 00:38:12.920
So combining those tools, we can
now do a sprint-based approach.

00:38:12.920 --> 00:38:15.230
We can now break a
data warehouse down

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:20.000
into many small sprints where we
can run each sprint in parallel

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:22.490
on both systems for
a period of time,

00:38:22.490 --> 00:38:26.030
and only move those users
over for that sprint

00:38:26.030 --> 00:38:29.153
when we are confident that
both systems are matching.

00:38:29.153 --> 00:38:31.070
The data's matching, the
queries are matching,

00:38:31.070 --> 00:38:33.500
the outputs a matching,
and performance is

00:38:33.500 --> 00:38:34.622
at least matching.

00:38:34.622 --> 00:38:36.080
And so when we've
done that, we can

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.372
move it, and move the next
sprint, and the next sprint.

00:38:38.372 --> 00:38:40.520
So it's not a big-bang
lift and shift anymore

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:43.610
because that's way too
difficult, too scary.

00:38:43.610 --> 00:38:45.230
We want to break it
down into sprints.

00:38:45.230 --> 00:38:47.761
And now we can do that.

00:38:47.761 --> 00:38:50.040
Now costs, are
they really lower?

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:51.120
People ask that.

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:53.850
Hadoop was pretty cheap
for storage on-premise.

00:38:53.850 --> 00:38:56.900
But, of course, we're
now dealing with storage

00:38:56.900 --> 00:39:01.090
on cloud between $0.01 and
$0.03 per gigabyte per month,

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.480
and getting lower all the time.

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:06.900
And that is impossible to
match with any on-site storage

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:08.430
that I know of.

00:39:08.430 --> 00:39:11.380
The separation of
compute and storage,

00:39:11.380 --> 00:39:13.273
so we now have storage
that's very low cost.

00:39:13.273 --> 00:39:14.940
We can bring different
computer engines.

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:17.987
We can bring BQ, we can bring
Spark, we can bring Python,

00:39:17.987 --> 00:39:19.570
we can bring whatever
computer engines

00:39:19.570 --> 00:39:22.960
we want to the same copy of
data on the same storage,

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:24.400
and the storage is low cost.

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:29.490
So there are significant cost
savings in a well-structured--

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:33.390
like Jas' new architecture is
a perfect example of a very

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:36.780
clean, simple architecture,
single point of truth,

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:40.110
under the low-cost storage,
and bringing any compute engine

00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:42.055
to that that you need.

00:39:42.055 --> 00:39:43.097
And that's the way to go.

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:47.790
So the promises to
take the frustration

00:39:47.790 --> 00:39:50.910
and cost out of these
migrations and the anxiety

00:39:50.910 --> 00:39:52.420
of these large migrations.

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:54.900
So you may have thousands--
well, you will have,

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:57.940
in some cases, many thousands
of users using these legacy

00:39:57.940 --> 00:39:58.440
systems.

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:01.020
We have to move them
without all the anxiety.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:02.970
And that's why we
approach this in this sort

00:40:02.970 --> 00:40:06.880
of sprint-wise process.

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:08.220
So we want to optimize it.

00:40:08.220 --> 00:40:09.210
We want to automate it.

00:40:09.210 --> 00:40:13.060
We want to use the tools to
do the conversion of code.

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:14.970
We want to do the
automated tools

00:40:14.970 --> 00:40:17.520
for doing the validation
and the data migration.

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:19.650
At right at the bottom, we
want to run in parallel,

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:21.810
reduce risk, and
errors and mistakes.

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:25.770
That is the only way we know of
moving these large environments

00:40:25.770 --> 00:40:28.180
successfully.

00:40:28.180 --> 00:40:31.350
So the targets of this
level of automation

00:40:31.350 --> 00:40:34.340
are here on the right,
clearly a lot of Teradata.

00:40:34.340 --> 00:40:37.050
The Teradata is the most
expensive, of course,

00:40:37.050 --> 00:40:37.800
data warehouse.

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:41.647
And moving that has the
biggest ROI of all of them.

00:40:41.647 --> 00:40:43.230
Netezza, if you're
a Netezza customer,

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:45.580
you know that IBM has
end-of-life support,

00:40:45.580 --> 00:40:47.760
a whole a lot of Netezza
machines at the moment.

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:50.940
There's a lot of people are
wanting to move Netezza.

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:53.132
Oracle, of course,
and Hadoop are still--

00:40:53.132 --> 00:40:54.840
as Jas mentioned,
there's a lot of Hadoop

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:58.230
that needs to be moved, and
even the old DB2 and Greenplum.

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:00.540
So there's a lot of these
tools have been written

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:04.770
to automate the migration
of these to cloud

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:08.475
and all the associated
pieces of ETL.

00:41:08.475 --> 00:41:10.860
So the time efficiency
now, through automation,

00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:15.570
we're seeing about a 2/3
reduction in cost and time.

00:41:15.570 --> 00:41:18.570
Time particularly because we
can automate a lot of this,

00:41:18.570 --> 00:41:20.660
automate the assessment,
automate the migration,

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:22.830
automate the
validation, and do it

00:41:22.830 --> 00:41:26.700
in a sprint-wise
process in about 50%

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:28.800
cost saving or a
little more sometimes.

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:31.590
So now we can move
things quicker

00:41:31.590 --> 00:41:35.600
and see the benefits that you
saw from the Kohl's example.

00:41:35.600 --> 00:41:37.350
So we're going to do
some Q&amp;A in a minute.

00:41:37.350 --> 00:41:40.105
I think, though, Sam you want
to come back for a minute?

00:41:43.430 --> 00:41:44.855
SAMRAT SAHA: Thank you.

00:41:47.710 --> 00:41:51.080
Yeah, so thanks, Dr. Phil.

00:41:51.080 --> 00:41:54.200
As you can see that
when we started off,

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:55.970
we talked about a
customer who was

00:41:55.970 --> 00:41:57.570
able to deliver on their own.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.540
And sometimes you
need a partner.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:02.030
Some of the key points that
stood out in what Dr. Phil just

00:42:02.030 --> 00:42:04.970
said, one is automation.

00:42:04.970 --> 00:42:06.890
A lot of this is
dependent on automation.

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:09.140
Can you get things
to be reliable

00:42:09.140 --> 00:42:11.780
and can be done quicker?

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:12.943
The other was validation.

00:42:12.943 --> 00:42:15.360
How do you validate whether
you've been successful or not?

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:17.410
So those are two really
important points.

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:18.650
And the third is--

00:42:18.650 --> 00:42:20.500
he did mention crashing
data warehouses.

00:42:20.500 --> 00:42:23.213
So that is definitely scary.

00:42:23.213 --> 00:42:24.380
So bringing it all together.

00:42:24.380 --> 00:42:26.230
So when I started
off, when I tried

00:42:26.230 --> 00:42:30.270
to distill what Jas had
just said about especially

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:33.510
their approach, we talked
about deciding to move,

00:42:33.510 --> 00:42:37.200
getting stakeholder buy-in,
identify all data sources,

00:42:37.200 --> 00:42:38.605
POC execute.

00:42:38.605 --> 00:42:39.480
Well, that's a start.

00:42:42.130 --> 00:42:44.560
This is the next
steps that you're

00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:47.065
going to take, move
historical data over, point

00:42:47.065 --> 00:42:50.640
our sources directly
to GCP, in this case,

00:42:50.640 --> 00:42:53.750
update your tools and
reports to point to BQ,

00:42:53.750 --> 00:42:56.170
evaluate performance,
and benchmark it,

00:42:56.170 --> 00:42:58.210
make sure that you're
getting identical results

00:42:58.210 --> 00:43:01.840
across identical data
sets, and yes, turn off

00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:05.770
on-prem EDW because that was
the goal at the beginning of it.

00:43:05.770 --> 00:43:08.740
Keep building and
making great things.

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:11.410
So it's complex.

00:43:11.410 --> 00:43:12.100
It's intense.

00:43:12.100 --> 00:43:14.620
And I've seen this happen.

00:43:14.620 --> 00:43:15.620
But it's doable.

00:43:15.620 --> 00:43:20.080
I mean, prime examples, Dr.
Phil has helped a lot of people

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:21.220
move over.

00:43:21.220 --> 00:43:22.900
I've seen Kohl's
move over as well.

00:43:22.900 --> 00:43:24.610
So it's definitely doable.

00:43:24.610 --> 00:43:27.410
It just takes effort.

00:43:27.410 --> 00:43:29.410
So we're going to go in
a Q&amp;A section right now.

00:43:29.410 --> 00:43:30.702
I'm going act as the moderator.

00:43:30.702 --> 00:43:32.202
So if you have any
questions, please

00:43:32.202 --> 00:43:33.850
feel free to step up
to the mic, and we

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:35.942
can answer your questions.

00:43:42.746 --> 00:43:44.750
MAN 1: It's not working here.

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:47.282
SAMRAT SAHA: Somebody
turn that mic on.

00:43:47.282 --> 00:43:48.740
MAN 2: Plug it in?

00:43:48.740 --> 00:43:49.410
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:43:49.410 --> 00:43:50.090
SAMRAT SAHA: There you go.

00:43:50.090 --> 00:43:51.090
AUDIENCE: Yes, it works.

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:53.123
I have a question
for Datametica,

00:43:53.123 --> 00:43:54.415
if I pronounced this correctly.

00:43:54.415 --> 00:43:55.442
PHIL SHELLEY: Yeah.

00:43:55.442 --> 00:43:57.760
AUDIENCE: Can you
address a little bit

00:43:57.760 --> 00:44:01.440
how-- is that a static
workload migration that you do?

00:44:01.440 --> 00:44:04.630
And how do you deal with
context information?

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:08.910
So for example, we've seen
a lot of query differences

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:12.090
that are only there because
certain data types are there

00:44:12.090 --> 00:44:14.190
and certain
transformations need to be

00:44:14.190 --> 00:44:16.360
applied when that is true.

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:21.750
So it's not always possible
to do just a query A query B

00:44:21.750 --> 00:44:24.840
type of rewrite, but rather it
needs to be run in the context

00:44:24.840 --> 00:44:26.430
that the original query was run.

00:44:26.430 --> 00:44:28.350
And if you had to
do that, or if you

00:44:28.350 --> 00:44:30.930
had to deal with that,
how many times did

00:44:30.930 --> 00:44:35.070
you have to actually manually
go and help the system rewrite?

00:44:35.070 --> 00:44:36.402
PHIL SHELLEY: So there's--

00:44:36.402 --> 00:44:37.110
and you're right.

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:39.780
And also the data model may
need to change in some cases

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:42.540
because you're coming from
schemas on data warehouses

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:46.890
where they're not very friendly
to these big data environments.

00:44:46.890 --> 00:44:48.810
So there may be very
high numbers of joins,

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:50.477
there may be concurrent
reads and writes

00:44:50.477 --> 00:44:52.140
going on at high volumes.

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:54.570
So there are some
changes that have

00:44:54.570 --> 00:44:59.670
to be done to both code and
data model and user queries

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:00.480
in some cases.

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:03.840
Now, I would say that that's
getting less and less.

00:45:03.840 --> 00:45:09.210
The need to make changes is
going down quite quickly now

00:45:09.210 --> 00:45:11.790
because the early days
of the cloud tools,

00:45:11.790 --> 00:45:16.410
they were not tolerant at all of
the data structures and schemas

00:45:16.410 --> 00:45:18.410
that were on data warehouses.

00:45:18.410 --> 00:45:21.630
Now the BQ particularly
has advanced a lot.

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:24.390
So we're seeing less
and less changes

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:27.210
to make the same workload
move without changing

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:28.777
schemas in the code.

00:45:28.777 --> 00:45:30.360
But of course, if
you want to-- if you

00:45:30.360 --> 00:45:35.130
do want to change something,
change the formats of data,

00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:38.048
the schemes of data, and then
maybe performance tuning,

00:45:38.048 --> 00:45:39.590
there is a little
bit of manual work.

00:45:39.590 --> 00:45:42.720
So it depends on-- it depends.

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:45.720
So sometimes it's highly
automated, sometimes

00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:48.610
it's 25% or so manual
work and cleanup.

00:45:48.610 --> 00:45:52.020
But it's very
workload-dependent.

00:45:52.020 --> 00:45:54.750
But the degree of
changes we're seeing

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:58.697
is going down as BQ
becomes more tolerant of--

00:45:58.697 --> 00:46:00.280
AUDIENCE: Well, I
think sometimes it's

00:46:00.280 --> 00:46:02.150
just legacy stuff that nobody--

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:04.438
none of the modern
systems will implement.

00:46:04.438 --> 00:46:06.230
So those will always
have to be dealt with.

00:46:06.230 --> 00:46:07.897
PHIL SHELLEY: There
is some manual work.

00:46:07.897 --> 00:46:09.742
This is not entirely automated.

00:46:09.742 --> 00:46:12.634
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:46:12.634 --> 00:46:15.745
PHIL SHELLEY: Another one?

00:46:15.745 --> 00:46:16.720
Hard to see.

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:21.110
Somebody's coming down--
maybe two, maybe three.

00:46:21.110 --> 00:46:22.472
George?

00:46:22.472 --> 00:46:23.383
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:46:23.383 --> 00:46:24.550
Thanks for the presentation.

00:46:24.550 --> 00:46:28.630
I'm curious to know about the
end user experience, the types

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:32.080
of tools, how many
end users did you

00:46:32.080 --> 00:46:34.780
have to support in the
conversion, what their tool

00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:37.095
compatibility experience was.

00:46:37.095 --> 00:46:38.470
And then my second
question would

00:46:38.470 --> 00:46:42.610
be, what's your experience
at estimating slot counts,

00:46:42.610 --> 00:46:44.890
and what you thought
it would be versus what

00:46:44.890 --> 00:46:48.390
your forecast is going to be.

00:46:48.390 --> 00:46:51.220
JASVINDER SINGH: Yeah,
so talking about the slot

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:54.430
counts, so there is a
calculator that Google

00:46:54.430 --> 00:46:57.100
has which basically
helps you look

00:46:57.100 --> 00:47:00.580
at your current on-prem
systems and come up

00:47:00.580 --> 00:47:02.470
with some kind of an estimate.

00:47:02.470 --> 00:47:04.930
If we run a similar
workload in the cloud,

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:07.470
this is how much it will
take to run it again.

00:47:07.470 --> 00:47:09.290
You should take that
with a grain of salt

00:47:09.290 --> 00:47:13.750
because sometimes these numbers
are really big in some cases.

00:47:13.750 --> 00:47:17.620
And we will-- basically we
started off with certain use

00:47:17.620 --> 00:47:20.260
cases, and worked
with Google, and said,

00:47:20.260 --> 00:47:23.440
if we have to do
certain workloads,

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:27.320
how much do you think it will
basically be on those slots?

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:28.930
So we started off with that.

00:47:28.930 --> 00:47:32.230
And we started to bring
in more and more use cases

00:47:32.230 --> 00:47:33.660
and add to those slots.

00:47:33.660 --> 00:47:36.230
So we have not started off
with a very big number.

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:38.760
So we are going with
a smaller number.

00:47:38.760 --> 00:47:41.620
And at this point,
so it's halfway

00:47:41.620 --> 00:47:43.450
done through the migration.

00:47:43.450 --> 00:47:50.050
I think we are trending towards
just getting to a number which

00:47:50.050 --> 00:47:53.890
is going to add a lot of
value to our final migration

00:47:53.890 --> 00:47:56.368
in terms of cost.

00:47:56.368 --> 00:47:59.350
AUDIENCE: And then my
first part was about the--

00:47:59.350 --> 00:48:03.400
I guess the end user tools
and the types of tools

00:48:03.400 --> 00:48:05.750
that you've moved
on to the platform,

00:48:05.750 --> 00:48:08.860
like Tableau and MicroStrategy,
and what that experience was.

00:48:08.860 --> 00:48:12.850
Did you have to rewrite
reports to take advantage

00:48:12.850 --> 00:48:13.932
of the new data model?

00:48:13.932 --> 00:48:15.515
Or did they come
over more seamlessly?

00:48:15.515 --> 00:48:16.432
JASVINDER SINGH: Yeah.

00:48:16.432 --> 00:48:18.520
I think the approach
has been that we

00:48:18.520 --> 00:48:21.160
don't want to just
simply lift and shift

00:48:21.160 --> 00:48:22.670
whatever we have in the cloud--

00:48:22.670 --> 00:48:24.760
whatever we have
on-prem because that's

00:48:24.760 --> 00:48:27.670
going to be very difficult
to support in the longer

00:48:27.670 --> 00:48:31.330
run because how the data
is in sixth normal form

00:48:31.330 --> 00:48:36.100
or it's normalized in
some of our systems.

00:48:36.100 --> 00:48:40.060
As we bring it on, we're trying
to normalize it, denormalize it

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:41.540
as much as we can.

00:48:41.540 --> 00:48:46.000
And there is a change that
we are asking our partners

00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:48.370
and business
stakeholders to make

00:48:48.370 --> 00:48:50.750
as we are moving to the cloud.

00:48:50.750 --> 00:48:52.780
So in terms of
tools, yes, we have

00:48:52.780 --> 00:48:55.810
brought in some of our
reporting tools to the cloud

00:48:55.810 --> 00:48:57.310
and making that available.

00:48:57.310 --> 00:49:00.810
And we have also enabled a
lot of tools which will enable

00:49:00.810 --> 00:49:02.530
self-service on the cloud.

00:49:02.530 --> 00:49:06.580
And Dataprep is one of the
examples where we basically

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:11.470
provide certified data
sets to our stakeholders

00:49:11.470 --> 00:49:13.030
and let them have it from there.

00:49:13.030 --> 00:49:14.920
They can create
their own structures

00:49:14.920 --> 00:49:17.958
and create the reporting that
they need eventually from that.

00:49:17.958 --> 00:49:20.053
SAMRAT SAHA: We have time
for one more question.

00:49:20.053 --> 00:49:22.220
PHIL SHELLEY: I was going
to say one quick question.

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:24.130
A lot of the newer
migrations, though,

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:27.050
are trying to keep the same
data model and same tools

00:49:27.050 --> 00:49:28.970
because change management
on the human side

00:49:28.970 --> 00:49:30.300
is the biggest issue.

00:49:30.300 --> 00:49:34.080
So what we do is-- that
is entirely possible now.

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:36.553
But there's usually an
optimization phase afterwards.

00:49:36.553 --> 00:49:37.970
Where things are
not working well,

00:49:37.970 --> 00:49:40.120
there's some long running
queries, some issues.

00:49:40.120 --> 00:49:41.390
So there's a tuning phase.

00:49:41.390 --> 00:49:44.440
[THEME MUSIC PLAYING]

